diagnosis_text:
The network between PC0 (20.1.1.10) and PC12 (120.1.1.10) is currently healthy and fully functional. Exhaustive diagnostics confirmed end-to-end reachability with zero packet loss across 25 pings and a correct four-hop traceroute path (PC0 → r1 → r11 → r12 → PC12).

The forwarding plane is fully correct: r1 serves as the OSPF Area Border Router bridging backbone area 0 and area 3; r11 is the OSPF/EIGRP boundary ASBR that redistributes the EIGRP-learned 120.0.0.0/8 (PC12's subnet) into OSPF via an AS-external type-2 LSA; r1 receives this LSA and installs the route pointing to r11; r12 (PC12's gateway) holds EIGRP routes from r11 plus a correctly configured kernel static default route to r11 for return traffic. No ACL or firewall rules are blocking traffic on any transit device, and IP forwarding is enabled everywhere.

The user's complaint coincides with the lab being started approximately 13 minutes before diagnostics ran (FRR processes all show a 23:22 UTC start time). During the first few minutes after startup, while OSPF was completing its adjacency negotiation between r1 and r11 (DD exchange logged at 23:23:09 UTC), r1 had no route to 120.0.0.0/8 and all pings from PC0 would have been silently dropped. Once the OSPF adjacency reached Full state and the external LSA was installed (roughly 3–4 minutes after startup), connectivity was restored automatically. No persistent misconfiguration exists; the symptom was a transient routing-protocol convergence window inherent to the lab restart.
=== END ===